{
  "abstract": "A Boolean function f of n variables is BLF if f(x) is bit j of K + a_1 x_1 + ... + a_n x_n for some integers K, a_i and some bit position j. The development proves, for every n, that the floors of (K + a.x) / 2^j satisfy every parallelogram inequality on the cube, that every threshold function is BLF, and that BLF is closed under XOR with GF(2)-affine functions; and it proves the exact counts |BLF(n)| = 4, 16, 208 and 10368 for n = 1, 2, 3 and 4 by a verified pruned search checked by the kernel, that BLF(3) is exactly the threshold functions XOR GF(2)-affine functions, and that BLF(4) adds exactly the affine translates of bit 1 of x1 + x2 + x3 + x4, which is not itself threshold XOR affine. It also proves, for every n, that f is BLF iff f = floor(t + r.x) mod 2 for real t and r; the selector lemma (z AND T(x) is bit j of an integer affine form iff T has integer weights with sum of absolute values below 2^j), with a gated threshold showing that no bit position below 4 serves every BLF function of five variables; and, through a square identity, that two and three steps of Rule 110 are not BLF.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "Joseph S. Farland"
    }
